---
scope: extensions
intent: >
  The extensions folder exists so that a developer opening a `.aide` file in
  any mainstream editor sees it rendered as Markdown — syntax highlighting,
  preview pane, the same experience as a `.md` file. The `.aide` extension
  is the visible face of the AIDE convention, and if it looks like a plain
  text file the convention reads as amateur. This folder holds whatever
  bundled assets are required to make each supported editor treat `.aide`
  as Markdown, with per-editor strategies chosen according to what that
  editor's extensibility model actually allows.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - VS Code and its forks (Cursor, Windsurf, Copilot-in-VSCode) get a
      bundled `.vsix` extension that makes `.aide` files use the editor's
      built-in Markdown language, giving full syntax highlighting and a
      working preview pane with no grammar bundled.
    - Zed gets a configuration patch applied to `.zed/settings.json`, not an
      extension. The only path to rendered preview for `.aide` files in Zed
      is the user-settings file-type mapping, and `aide_init` is what applies it.
    - Per-editor choices are made based on what the editor's extensibility
      API actually supports, not on what would be consistent across editors.
      A Zed extension that "sort of works" is worse than no extension.
    - Each bundled asset is checked into the repo so `aide_init` can install
      it without a network round-trip, marketplace account, or build step.
  undesired:
    - A Zed extension that produces partial highlighting but no preview pane,
      because the developer's mental model is "extension installed = feature
      works" and a silently-missing preview button reads as a broken tool.
    - A VS Code extension that ships its own Markdown grammar instead of
      reusing the built-in one. Extra maintenance, worse highlighting over
      time as the editor's Markdown support evolves, and a preview pane that
      has to be re-wired by hand.
    - Assets that have to be built or published at install time. `aide_init`
      runs on a developer's machine, which may be offline, unauthenticated,
      or both. Anything that reaches for the network during init is wrong.
---

## Context

The `.aide` suffix was chosen because it is distinctive, memorable, and has no
collisions with existing formats — but the same properties mean no editor
recognizes it out of the box. Without IDE support, `.aide` files render as
plain unstyled text, and the whole convention starts to look like a hack.
This folder's job is to make the suffix visually first-class in every editor
the project supports, using whatever mechanism each editor actually allows.

This folder is the orchestrator surface for the IDE-integration domain of
aidemd-mcp: it holds the bundled assets that `aide_init` installs into each
supported editor. The installer itself is thin; the strategic decisions about
*what* to install per editor, and *why*, belong here next to the artifacts
themselves.

The research below is load-bearing for the Zed vs VS Code strategy split.
It is captured in this spec because the resulting decisions — "no Zed
extension, yes VS Code extension" — are counterintuitive, and without the
reasoning on file the question gets re-litigated every time someone new
looks at the folder.

## Strategy

**VS Code ships as a bundled extension because its extension API supports
exactly the mapping we need.** VS Code lets an extension declare that a new
file suffix should be handled by an existing built-in language. Declaring
`.aide` as belonging to the editor's built-in Markdown language is enough:
the built-in grammar, highlighting, and preview pane all apply automatically,
with no grammar bundled and no maintenance cost as the editor's Markdown
support evolves. The packaged `.vsix` is checked into the repo so `aide_init`
can install it locally without touching the marketplace, and the same
package works for every VS Code fork that speaks the same extension API —
Cursor, Windsurf, Copilot-in-VSCode.

**Zed ships as a config patch because its extension API cannot produce a
preview pane.** Zed's Markdown preview is a hardcoded built-in view that is
gated on a file's language being the built-in Markdown language — not on the
file's grammar, not on anything an extension can influence. Zed's extension API exposes grammars, language servers,
themes, and slash commands, but does not expose custom rendered views. No
extension, no matter how it is shaped, can produce a preview pane for a
non-Markdown language. We verified this the hard way: every extension shape
we tried — custom language pointed at the Markdown grammar, bundled query
files copied from Zed's own Markdown support, reusing the Markdown grammar
by name — produced the same outcome. Highlighting ranged from broken to
partial to working, and the preview button never appeared in any
configuration, because preview is gated on the language identity, not on
grammar. The only path to rendered preview is the user-settings file-type
mapping, which tells Zed to treat `.aide` files **as Markdown files** —
same language, same preview, same everything. `aide_init` merges this
setting into `.zed/settings.json` at init time, and there is no Zed
extension to publish, maintain, or ship.

**Per-editor strategies beat cross-editor consistency.** The instinct to
ship one extension per editor in a consistent shape is wrong here. Zed and
VS Code have genuinely different extensibility models, and a consistent
shape would force either a broken Zed extension or a VS-Code-as-settings-patch
approach that throws away the parts of the VS Code extension API that
actually work. The right answer is to match each editor's real capability
and let `aide_init` handle the divergence — the developer experience is
identical across editors even though the mechanism underneath is not.

**Everything ships in-repo, nothing ships over the network.** The `.vsix`
is checked in so `aide_init` on a freshly-cloned machine can install it
without a network call, a marketplace account, or a build step. New
versions bump the filename, so the init tool can pick the latest and
install idempotently. Publishing to a marketplace is a maintainer
activity, not an end-user activity, and `aide_init` must never depend on it.

## Good examples

A developer clones a project and runs `aide_init`. They see:

> Configured .zed/settings.json — `.aide` will render as Markdown on next open.
> Installed VS Code extension aide-markdown-0.0.1.

They open a `.aide` file in Zed: full syntax highlighting, the preview pane
opens, headings and lists render the same as any `.md` file in the project.
They open the same file in Cursor: full syntax highlighting, the preview
pane opens, same result. Two editors, two mechanisms underneath, one
consistent experience from the developer's seat.

A developer who already has a heavily customized `.zed/settings.json` runs
`aide_init` a second time. The configurator reads the existing file, sees
that the Markdown file-type mapping already includes `.aide`, and writes
nothing. Their custom theme, keybindings, and language settings are all
untouched, and the init run reports the Zed step as already configured.

A developer who has never run `aide_init` opens a `.aide` file in VS Code
after installing the bundled extension by hand. The file is immediately
recognized as Markdown: the language indicator in the status bar reads
Markdown, the preview shortcut opens a working preview pane, and the
outline view populates from the headings. No additional steps, no extra
settings, no "enable preview for this file type" prompt.

## Bad examples

A developer installs a Zed extension for AIDE, opens a `.aide` file, and
sees partially working syntax highlighting — headings are colored but code
fences are not, or vice versa. They look for a preview button and find
none. They right-click, search the command palette for "preview", and turn
up nothing applicable. Their conclusion is that either the extension is
half-finished or the AIDE convention itself is amateur, and they stop
using `.aide` files on that machine. The extension technically installed
and technically did something, and that is exactly why it is worse than
shipping no Zed extension at all.

A developer installs a VS Code extension for AIDE that ships its own
Markdown grammar and registers `.aide` as a brand-new language. Highlighting
looks almost right but subtly lags the editor's built-in Markdown — a new
Markdown feature lands in VS Code and `.aide` files do not get it. The
preview shortcut does nothing on `.aide` files because the built-in preview
is bound to the built-in Markdown language, not to this extension's custom
one. The developer has to add a custom keybinding, or manually switch the
file's language in the status bar every time they open one. Every friction
point here is self-inflicted: the editor offered a zero-cost mapping to its
own Markdown language and the extension declined it.

A developer runs `aide_init` on a laptop with no network access. The tool
tries to publish or download the VS Code extension from a marketplace,
fails on the network call, and aborts before configuring Zed. The
developer is left with a half-initialized project and an error message
about missing credentials they should never have needed. Init is supposed
to be a local, offline, idempotent operation — anything that reaches for
the network during init has already failed its contract.
